# Files that are read by something that does not forgive a carriage return.
#
# A checkout on Windows with core.autocrlf on turns every text file's line
# endings into CRLF. A shell script copied into a Linux image that way fails
# with "not found" on its own first line, and a systemd unit with them is
# refused. These stay LF whatever the machine.
*.sh            text eol=lf
# Windows PowerShell 5.1 reads a script without a byte-order mark as the machine's ANSI code page, and every
# one of these has one (scripts/check-repo.mjs holds that). CRLF is what a Windows editor writes back.
*.ps1           text eol=crlf
*.service       text eol=lf
Dockerfile      text eol=lf
*.yml           text eol=lf
*.yaml          text eol=lf
Caddyfile       text eol=lf
*.caddyfile     text eol=lf
*.tmpl          text eol=lf
*.conf          text eol=lf
